// Iteration end-to-end (Phase 2.2d) — the increment's gate.
//
// Run three ways: `nova run` (JIT), `nova build` (object backend), and
// `NOVA_GC_STRESS=1`. The stress run is not belt-and-braces here either: a
// `.filter().map()` chain is a *tower* of records, so the backing array is
// reachable only as `MapIter` -> `FilterIter` -> `VecIter` -> `Vec` -> `data`
// while every `next` allocates a fresh `Option` (and `collect` grows a `Vec`).
// A collector whose root scan misses one link in that tower loses elements
// silently rather than crashing.
//
// Nothing in this file may panic — a panic aborts the process and silently
// truncates the rest of the gate, so every `Option` is opened with
// `unwrap_or`/`is_some`/`is_none` or a `match`, never `unwrap`. Same
// convention as `tests/runtime/assoc_types.nova`.
//
// Maps to the plan's Task 5 Step 1 gate list item by item:
//   1. `for` over a `Vec` via `.iter()`                       -> FOR_VEC
//   2. `for` over an integer range, STILL WORKING             -> FOR_RANGE
//   3. exhaustion, then one further `next()` is `None`        -> EXHAUST
//   4. an empty source whose FIRST `next()` is `None`         -> EMPTY
//   5. a two-stage `.filter().map()` chain, adapter-on-adapter -> CHAIN
//   6. all six default methods                                -> SIX
//   7. every generic block at `Bool` or at `Float`            -> BOOL, FLOAT
//
// Item 2 is not filler. Task 2 rewrote the very function that dispatches a
// `for` head (`check_for_iterator` beside `check_for`, in
// `crates/nova-typeck/src/check.rs`), so the range form is exactly the thing
// that change could have broken without any new feature noticing.
//
// **Item 7 is the most important thing in this file, and it is not
// decoration.** `mir_ty` maps `Int` AND `Char` to `MirTy::I64`, and
// `String`/`Fn`/`Record`/`Sum`/`Array` to `MirTy::Ptr` — `pointer_type()`,
// i.e. `types::I64` on x86-64. So at the level a backend can see, `Int`,
// `Char`, `String` and every heap type are the SAME type, and an item type
// resolved to the wrong one of them is invisible after lowering. Only `Bool`
// (`I8`) and `Float` (`F64`) have machine classes a wrong answer cannot hide
// in. An `Int`-only iterator fixture cannot see a wrong `Item` at the
// monomorphization seam at all; `tests/runtime/assoc_types.nova`'s header
// records the measurement that established this.
//
// Mutations run against this file, each applied to the shipped compiler and
// each confirmed to make the fixture fail under all three configurations.
// Recorded because a fixture line that survives a broken compiler is worse
// than no line — it reads as coverage. The line named is where the wrong value
// appears, not merely where the count moves.
//   A. `Vec::iter`'s cursor start, `i: 0` -> `i: 1`
//      (`std/collections/lib.nova`)                          -> FOR_VEC,
//                                                               EXHAUST, SIX,
//                                                               BOOL, FLOAT
//   B. `FilterIter::next`'s `if p(n.unwrap())` -> `if true`  -> CHAIN (all
//                                                               three drive
//                                                               forms), SIX,
//                                                               BOOL, FLOAT
//   C. `Iterator::count`'s `n = n + 1` deleted               -> EXHAUST
//                                                               (consumed),
//                                                               SIX, BOOL,
//                                                               FLOAT
//   D. monomorphization's normalization caching its first answer
//      process-wide (`Specializer::ty`, `crates/nova-mir/src/mono.rs`)
//                                                            -> FLOAT ONLY
//
// **Mutation D is the one that justifies item 7, and its result is sharper
// than expected.** An `Int`-only reduction of this file — items 1 to 6 with
// the BOOL and FLOAT sections deleted — survives it **byte-identically**,
// which is the measurement item 7 exists to answer. And of the two
// distinguishable classes only `Float` catches it: the `Bool` lines pass under
// mutation D unchanged. So the `Float` half of item 7 is not redundant with
// the `Bool` half, and a `Bool`-only item 7 would have shipped this fixture
// with a hole.
//
// `tests/runtime/assoc_types.nova`'s header records the converse for *its*
// mutation C, where adding a `Vec<Bool>` was what killed it — but note that
// its mutation C and this file's mutation D are the SAME mutation (mono's
// normalization caching its first answer). So the variable that flips which
// class catches it is the **usage shape**, not the mutation, and the ordering
// between the two classes is not symmetric:
//   - `Specializer::ty` early-returns on `!substituted.has_assoc()`
//     (`crates/nova-mir/src/mono.rs:337`), so only a projection-BEARING type
//     reaches normalization at all — a shape that never puts a projection on
//     the wire cannot observe the mutation from any instantiation.
//   - `Option<X>` is a `Ty::Sum`, which `mir_ty` maps to `MirTy::Ptr` for
//     EVERY `X`, so a projection reached only through an `Option` hides a
//     wrong answer completely regardless of the class inside it.
//   - `Bool` is `MirTy::I8`, and its only two values 0 and 1 survive an `I64`
//     confusion intact in the low byte of a GPR; `Float` is `MirTy::F64` and
//     CROSSES REGISTER BANKS.
// So `Float` is strictly stronger than `Bool` here, and `Bool` silently agrees
// with `Int` whenever the confusion happens to be value-preserving. Carry both
// anyway: `Bool` is the class `assoc_types` measured its own kill on, so
// dropping it would discard a measurement rather than a duplicate. But the half
// that must never be trimmed is the `Float` one.
//
// Two mutations were designed for this file and **discarded as unfalsifiable**
// rather than reported as passes, because each breaks something unrelated
// first and its failure is indistinguishable from a different bug:
//   - The exclusive range's condition, `BinOp::Lt` -> `BinOp::Le` in
//     `check_for` — intended for FOR_RANGE. `for i in 0..len` is load-bearing
//     throughout std — the inline grow inside `Vec::push`, `Debug for
//     String`'s `dbg`, and `chars_to_string`, `reverse`, `split`, `join` and
//     `repeat` in `std/strings` — so it aborts inside std before this fixture
//     prints a single line, and it fails `collections_run`, `strings_run`,
//     `std_core_run`, `for_loops_run` and `assoc_types_run` identically.
//     FOR_RANGE is still correct to have as a regression guard — Task 2 edited
//     the function that dispatches a `for` head — but no mutation this fixture
//     *discriminates* was found for it, and the reason is that the range form
//     cannot regress quietly in the first place.
//   - `MapIter::next` forwarding its inner item unmapped (`Some(n.unwrap())`)
//     — intended for item 7. It does not type-check inside `std/core` at all
//     (`E0010`: `next` should return `Option<T1>` but its body has type
//     `Option<T0::Item>`), so every program fails identically and nothing about
//     this fixture is on trial.
//
// One authoring note, since it will bite the next person: a closure passed to
// `fold` needs its parameters ANNOTATED when the body is arithmetic that works
// at more than one NUMERIC type. `fs.iter().fold(0.0, |a, x| a + x)` over a
// `Vec<Float>` is `E0010: argument has type 'fn(Int, Int) -> Int' but
// 'fn(Float, Float) -> Float' was expected` — the expected type is right and
// the closure's own inference defaulted to `Int`. `|a: Float, x: Float|`
// fixes it. Pre-existing, unrelated to this increment, and the reason the
// `Float` folds below carry annotations while the `map`/`filter` closures do
// not (their bodies mention a `Float` literal, which pins them).
//
// The rule is exactly that narrow, so do not generalise it into "annotate
// every `fold`": the `Bool` folds below deliberately carry NO annotations.
// `||` and `&&` are not numeric and admit only `Bool`, so there is no second
// candidate type to default to — `bs.iter().fold(false, |a, b| a || b)` and
// `fold(true, |a, b| a && b)` over a `Vec<Bool>` compile as written and print
// `or=true and=false`. They were annotated in an earlier draft of this file
// for symmetry with the `Float` folds; the annotations were measured
// unnecessary and removed, with this `.stdout` byte-identical either way.

// A hand-written `Iterator` that is not a `VecIter`, so `for` and the six
// methods see an implementor whose `Item` is not tied to a container's element
// type. Also the only shape that can expose its own cursor, which is how
// EXHAUST observes that a consumed iterator stays consumed.
record Countdown { n: Int }

impl Iterator for Countdown {
    type Item = Int
    fn next(mut self) -> Option<Self::Item> {
        if self.n <= 0 { return None }
        self.n = self.n - 1
        Some(self.n)
    }
}

fn main() {
    // `unwrap_or`'s sentinel, bound to a local because a line starting with
    // `-` after a block is absorbed as binary subtraction.
    let miss = 0 - 1

    // === FOR_VEC — item 1: `for` over a `Vec` via `.iter()`. There is no
    // `IntoIterator`, so `for x in v` does not work and `.iter()` is the
    // spelling; the call result is the `for` head directly, with no
    // intermediate binding, because that is what a user writes.
    let mut v: Vec<Int> = Vec::new()
    v.push(10)
    v.push(20)
    v.push(40)
    let mut seen = ""
    for x in v.iter() { seen = "${seen}${x}," }
    println("for_vec ${seen}")

    // A user implementor through the same desugar, so `for` is not pinned only
    // against std's own `VecIter`.
    let cd = Countdown { n: 3 }
    let mut cdseq = ""
    for x in cd { cdseq = "${cdseq}${x}," }
    println("for_user ${cdseq}")

    // === FOR_RANGE — item 2: the integer-range form, STILL WORKING. Task 2
    // edited the function that dispatches a `for` head, so this is a
    // regression guard, not a feature demo. Both an ascending range and an
    // empty one (`3..3`), since the empty case is where an off-by-one in the
    // range lowering shows up.
    let mut r = ""
    for i in 0..4 { r = "${r}${i}," }
    println("for_range ${r}")
    let mut r2 = "none"
    for i in 3..3 { r2 = "${r2}${i}," }
    println("for_range_empty ${r2}")

    // === EXHAUST — item 3: iterate to the end, then confirm ONE FURTHER
    // `next()` is `None`. Six `next()` calls over three elements rather than
    // stopping at the first `None`, so `None` REPEATING is pinned and a
    // non-advancing `next` is observable instead of hanging. Values 10/20/40
    // rather than 10/20/30 because with 10/20/30 a non-advancing iterator sums
    // to the same 60 over six turns and only `nones` would notice.
    let mut it = v.iter()
    let mut total = 0
    let mut nones = 0
    let mut k = 0
    while k < 6 {
        match it.next() {
            Some(x) => total = total + x,
            None => nones = nones + 1,
        }
        k = k + 1
    }
    println("exhaust total=${total} nones=${nones}")

    // The same property on a consumer rather than by hand: `count` drives to
    // exhaustion, and because `let mut it2 = self` inside it ALIASES the
    // caller's record (records are heap objects, no copy semantics), the
    // second `count` sees an already-consumed iterator. `0`, not `3`.
    let mut cd2 = Countdown { n: 3 }
    let first_count = cd2.count()
    println("exhaust_consumed first=${first_count} again=${cd2.count()}")

    // === EMPTY — item 4: a `Vec::new()` whose FIRST `next()` is already
    // `None`. A separate behaviour from exhausting a non-empty source, because
    // the cursor never advances at all — the guard is reached with
    // `i == len == 0`, an index EXHAUST never visits. The annotation is
    // required: an empty `Vec::new()` has nothing to infer its element type
    // from.
    let mut e: Vec<Int> = Vec::new()
    let mut ei = e.iter()
    let e0 = ei.next()
    println("empty first_is_none=${e0.is_none()} value=${e0.unwrap_or(miss)}")
    // And through the two forms a user reaches for.
    let mut e2: Vec<Int> = Vec::new()
    println("empty count=${e2.iter().count()} any=${e2.iter().any(|n| n > 0)}")
    let mut eseen = "none"
    for x in e2.iter() { eseen = "${eseen}${x}," }
    println("empty for=${eseen}")

    // === CHAIN — item 5: a two-stage `.filter().map()`, proving
    // adapter-on-adapter: the outer `MapIter`'s `I` is a `FilterIter`, whose
    // own `type Item = I::Item` is itself a projection, so resolving the
    // outer adapter's `f: fn(I::Item) -> U` chases through two impls before
    // landing on `Int`. Driven three ways — `for`, a chained `collect`, and a
    // hand-driven `next` — because they are three different seams: the
    // desugar, a consumer's `let mut it = self`, and a direct receiver call.
    let mut cseen = ""
    for x in v.iter().filter(|n| n > 10).map(|n| n + 1) { cseen = "${cseen}${x}," }
    println("chain for=${cseen}")
    let cgot = v.iter().filter(|n| n > 10).map(|n| n + 1).collect()
    println("chain collect len=${cgot.len()} a=${cgot.get(0).unwrap_or(miss)} b=${cgot.get(1).unwrap_or(miss)} past=${cgot.get(2).unwrap_or(miss)}")
    // `it:` first, `keep:`/`f:` after — the order matters and is not style. A
    // record-literal field initializer is ORDER-SENSITIVE when a later field's
    // type names a projection on the parameter an earlier field pins: with `f`
    // written before `it`, `I` is not yet concrete when `f` is checked, so the
    // same literal is rejected. Measured on THIS block with the two fields
    // swapped: **exactly one `E0010`** (`field 'f' expects 'fn(?N::Item) -> ?M'
    // but found 'fn(Int) -> Int'`) and no second diagnostic.
    //
    // The `?N`/`?M` are deliberately not real numerals. An earlier version of
    // this comment quoted `?9`/`?10`, which were measured on a smaller program
    // and were never this block's — re-measured here, the same swap gives
    // `?45`/`?46`. An inference-variable index counts every variable `main`
    // allocated before that point, so *any* edit earlier in `main` renumbers
    // them, in a fixture whose whole purpose is to keep growing. The shape
    // (`fn(?N::Item) -> ?M`, an unresolved projection on an unsolved variable)
    // is the load-bearing part and the part that stays true.
    //
    // Whether that `E0010` also drags `E0011`s behind it is a property of the
    // surrounding shape, not of the swap, so do not carry a count from one
    // shape to another. The failed unification leaves `I` and `U` free, and
    // `E0011` fires only for the ones nothing later pins. Here nothing
    // cascades: `it: inner` still pins `I`, and the `next()`/`unwrap_or(miss)`
    // chain below pins `U`. The same swap in a literal that is bound and never
    // driven leaves both free — `let m = MapIter { f: |n| n + 1, it: v.iter()
    // }` then `let _ = m` measures one `E0010` and **six** `E0011`s. Pinned by
    // `a_record_field_initializer_is_order_sensitive_when_it_names_a_projection`
    // in `crates/nova-typeck/src/check.rs`, which is the undriven shape.
    //
    // Both are also bound to locals first because a record literal cannot be a
    // `for` head at all — `for x in NotIt { z: 1 }` is `P0001: expected
    // expression (in statement), found ':'`, and parenthesising does not help.
    let inner = FilterIter { it: v.iter(), keep: |n| n > 10 }
    let mut outer = MapIter { it: inner, f: |n| n + 1 }
    let n1 = outer.next()
    let n2 = outer.next()
    let n3 = outer.next()
    println("chain by_hand a=${n1.unwrap_or(miss)} b=${n2.unwrap_or(miss)} exhausted=${n3.is_none()}")

    // === SIX — item 6: all six default methods. Each consumer exhausts its
    // receiver, so each line gets its own fresh `.iter()`. Every one is
    // written as a chain onto a call result with no intermediate binding —
    // the form the increment was designed around, and the form that was
    // `E0060` until the four consumers moved from `mut self` to plain `self`.
    let sixmap = v.iter().map(|n| n + 1).collect()
    println("six map len=${sixmap.len()} a=${sixmap.get(0).unwrap_or(miss)} c=${sixmap.get(2).unwrap_or(miss)}")
    let sixfilter = v.iter().filter(|n| n > 15).collect()
    println("six filter len=${sixfilter.len()} a=${sixfilter.get(0).unwrap_or(miss)}")
    println("six fold=${v.iter().fold(0, |a, x| a + x)}")
    println("six count=${v.iter().count()}")
    println("six any_hit=${v.iter().any(|n| n > 30)} any_miss=${v.iter().any(|n| n > 90)}")
    let sixcollect = v.iter().collect()
    println("six collect len=${sixcollect.len()} b=${sixcollect.get(1).unwrap_or(miss)}")

    // === BOOL — item 7, first half. `Bool` is `MirTy::I8`, so every generic
    // block below is instantiated at a machine class `Int` cannot be mistaken
    // for: `VecIter<Bool>`, `MapIter<_, Bool>`, `FilterIter<VecIter<Bool>>`,
    // and all six methods. The `map` to `Bool` from an `Int` source is the
    // sharpest of these — the item type CHANGES partway down the pipeline, so
    // a `MapIter::next` that forwarded its inner item unmapped has an `I8`
    // hole to fall into rather than an `I64` one to hide in.
    let mut bs: Vec<Bool> = Vec::new()
    bs.push(true)
    bs.push(false)
    bs.push(true)
    let mut bseen = ""
    for b in bs.iter() { bseen = "${bseen}${b}," }
    println("bool for=${bseen}")
    println("bool count=${bs.iter().count()} any_hit=${bs.iter().any(|b| b)} any_miss=${bs.iter().any(|b| !b && b)}")
    println("bool fold_or=${bs.iter().fold(false, |a, b| a || b)} fold_and=${bs.iter().fold(true, |a, b| a && b)}")
    let bfilter = bs.iter().filter(|b| b).collect()
    println("bool filter len=${bfilter.len()} a=${bfilter.get(0).unwrap_or(false)} b=${bfilter.get(1).unwrap_or(false)} past=${bfilter.get(2).unwrap_or(false)}")
    let bnot = bs.iter().map(|b| !b).collect()
    println("bool map a=${bnot.get(0).unwrap_or(true)} b=${bnot.get(1).unwrap_or(false)} c=${bnot.get(2).unwrap_or(true)}")
    let bchain = bs.iter().filter(|b| b).map(|b| !b).collect()
    println("bool chain len=${bchain.len()} a=${bchain.get(0).unwrap_or(true)} b=${bchain.get(1).unwrap_or(true)} past=${bchain.get(2).unwrap_or(true)}")
    // `Int` source -> `Bool` items: the item type changes mid-pipeline.
    let itob = v.iter().map(|n| n > 15).collect()
    println("bool from_int a=${itob.get(0).unwrap_or(true)} b=${itob.get(1).unwrap_or(false)} c=${itob.get(2).unwrap_or(false)}")
    println("bool from_int any=${v.iter().map(|n| n > 15).any(|b| b)} count=${v.iter().map(|n| n > 15).count()}")
    let mut bhand = bs.iter()
    let bn = bhand.next()
    println("bool by_hand some=${bn.is_some()} value=${bn.unwrap_or(false)}")

    // === FLOAT — item 7, second half. `Float` is `MirTy::F64`, the only other
    // machine class in the language, and unlike `Bool` it is a *wider*
    // register class — a wrong `Item` here is a Cranelift type error rather
    // than a silently truncated one. Values are chosen so every printed result
    // keeps a visible fractional part (`0.5`/`1.25`/`2.25`), because a `Float`
    // whose result happens to be integral prints as `3`, not `3.0`, and would
    // read identically to a wrongly-`Int` answer.
    let mut fs: Vec<Float> = Vec::new()
    fs.push(0.5)
    fs.push(1.25)
    fs.push(2.25)
    let mut fseen = ""
    for x in fs.iter() { fseen = "${fseen}${x}," }
    println("float for=${fseen}")
    println("float count=${fs.iter().count()} any_hit=${fs.iter().any(|x| x > 2.0)} any_miss=${fs.iter().any(|x| x > 9.0)}")
    // Init `0.25`, not `0.0`: the three elements sum to exactly `4.0`, which
    // prints as `4` and would read identically to a wrongly-`Int` answer. With
    // `0.25` the total is `4.25` and the fractional part is on the page.
    println("float fold=${fs.iter().fold(0.25, |a: Float, x: Float| a + x)}")
    let ffilter = fs.iter().filter(|x| x > 1.0).collect()
    println("float filter len=${ffilter.len()} a=${ffilter.get(0).unwrap_or(9.75)} b=${ffilter.get(1).unwrap_or(9.75)} past=${ffilter.get(2).unwrap_or(9.75)}")
    let fmapped = fs.iter().map(|x| x + 0.25).collect()
    println("float map a=${fmapped.get(0).unwrap_or(9.75)} b=${fmapped.get(1).unwrap_or(9.75)} c=${fmapped.get(2).unwrap_or(9.75)}")
    let fchain = fs.iter().filter(|x| x > 1.0).map(|x| x + 0.25).collect()
    println("float chain len=${fchain.len()} a=${fchain.get(0).unwrap_or(9.75)} b=${fchain.get(1).unwrap_or(9.75)} past=${fchain.get(2).unwrap_or(9.75)}")
    // `Int` source -> `Float` items, and `Float` source -> `Bool` items, so
    // both directions across the two distinguishable classes are exercised.
    // The closure READS its `Int` argument (`if n > 15`) rather than returning
    // a constant: Nova has no `as` casts, so an `Int` cannot be converted to a
    // `Float`, and a constant-returning `|n| 0.5` would type-check without the
    // source's items ever being consulted — proving nothing about the seam.
    let itof = v.iter().map(|n| if n > 15 { 1.25 } else { 0.25 }).collect()
    println("float from_int len=${itof.len()} a=${itof.get(0).unwrap_or(9.75)} b=${itof.get(1).unwrap_or(9.75)} c=${itof.get(2).unwrap_or(9.75)}")
    println("float from_int fold=${v.iter().map(|n| if n > 15 { 1.25 } else { 0.25 }).fold(0.0, |a: Float, x: Float| a + x)}")
    let ftob = fs.iter().map(|x| x > 1.0).collect()
    println("float to_bool a=${ftob.get(0).unwrap_or(true)} b=${ftob.get(1).unwrap_or(false)} c=${ftob.get(2).unwrap_or(false)}")
    let mut fhand = fs.iter()
    let fn1 = fhand.next()
    println("float by_hand some=${fn1.is_some()} value=${fn1.unwrap_or(9.75)}")
}
